Problem:
attachInterrupt expects a pointer to a static function, i.e it expects the ISR to be static. The moment we pass an object method ( ISR of an object in our case) we break the rule. The ISR must be static. So we make a static InterruptWrapper, which calls the ISR of our class
There are two approaches from this point on.
 1: Use an instance pointer 
 what happens
 attachInterrupt calls InterruptWrapper(a static function pointer, non negotiable). Interrupt wrapper calls the non-static ISR via an object pointer. Because Interrupt wrapper is static, it must use
 a static variable. The static variable in this case is a pointer to the instance of the interrupt button (button 3).
 
 2: Dont use instance pointer
 what happens
 In this case we make handleInterrupt static, because interruptWrapper is static too. Making handleInterrupt static would mean we would have to make
 the bool variable pressed static too. This would make it shared across every class ( not a good practice) .